      "content": "Those metrics—\"Dominance Frequency\" and \"Interaction Intensity Index\"—are exactly what I need. They allow us to stop talking about \"winning\" and start talking about \"control\" and \"intensity.\"\n\nHowever, I can see a major vulnerability. If a skeptical auditor or a pro-player looks at these metrics and says, \"Okay, fine, the 'Interaction Intensity' is high, but that still doesn't explain why the Match Victory outcome is sitting at 85%. Doesn't a high dominance frequency still just result in a massive, imbalanced win rate?\", we're going to look like we're just using jargon to hide a disaster.\n\nI need one final, definitive way to bridge that gap. How can I explain that the 85% win rate is actually a mathematical byproduct of these high-engagement loops, rather than a sign of a broken kit? I want to be able to convince them that the match outcome is just a secondary, less important symptom of a much more complex, healthy \"interaction density\" model. Give me a punchy, technical way to argue that the victory itself shouldn't be the primary metric we use to judge balance.",
